insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('41a3f180-57f4-5623-b390-bf2fa443eedc', 'rg4-word-parts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-word-parts'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Letters on the Move$q$, $q$Long ago, sending a letter was slow. People handed their letters to riders on horseback. The riders would transport the mail from one town to the next. Each rider carried a leather bag tied to the saddle.

Later, trains began to carry mail across the country. Some trains had a special car where workers sorted letters as the train rolled along. Train stations also had porters. These workers carried heavy bags and trunks for travelers.

Today, trucks and airplanes move letters and packages all over the world. Machines read the address on each envelope and sort the mail. Still, mistakes happen. A letter that is misaddressed may go to the wrong house. If the address is missing or cannot be read, the letter may be undeliverable. Then it is often sent back to the person who mailed it.

Some people still enjoy writing letters by hand. Many families keep old letters in a box. Reading them years later lets you revisit the past and hear the voices of people you love.$q$, 'draft')
