-- Passage rg5-argument-evidence-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ded3b7fb-a490-5d40-ac8b-083f907f54a1', 'rg5-argument-evidence-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$A Share Table for Our Cafeteria$q$, $q$Every day in our cafeteria, students toss unopened milk cartons, whole apples and sealed packs of crackers into the trash. Much of this food is perfectly good. Our school should set up a share table, where students can leave food they will not eat for others to take.

A share table would cut down on waste. For two weeks, our fifth grade class counted the unopened items thrown away at lunch. We counted an average of 85 items each day. Over a school year, that adds up to more than 15,000 items.

A share table would also help hungry students. Some students finish their lunch and still feel hungry. Others skip breakfast at home. Ms. Haddad, our school nurse, says several students visit her office each week with stomachaches from hunger. An extra apple or carton of milk could help them make it to the end of the day.

Share tables are simple to run. Hollis Elementary started one last fall. Their rules are short: only sealed packages and whole fruit with peels can go on the table. Lunch helpers wipe it down each day. Anything left over goes to a local food pantry.

Some people worry about germs. The Hollis rules solve that problem because nothing opened is allowed.

A share table would teach us to respect food and to care for each other. Let's start one this spring.$q$, 'draft')
