-- Passage rg5-argument-evidence-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7ccc10d3-882a-5353-b74d-1cf449a537ab', 'rg5-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Keep Instrument Lessons$q$, $q$Our school board is thinking about ending instrument lessons for fourth and fifth graders to save money. I believe that would be a mistake. Music lessons give students benefits that last far beyond the classroom.

Learning an instrument builds patience and hard work. A beginner cannot play a whole song on the first day. Students must practice the same few notes again and again until their fingers learn the pattern. Kwame Asante, a fifth grader in our band, practiced a hard trumpet part every night for three weeks before he played it without a mistake.

Music lessons also connect to other subjects. Reading music means counting beats and dividing measures into halves and quarters. That is the same thinking students use with fractions. At our school last year, band students scored an average of 8 points higher on the spring math test than the year before.

Band and orchestra bring students together. Players must listen to each other and keep the same tempo. Students who might never meet in class become friends in the music room.

Some board members say lessons cost too much. But our parent group has offered to raise half the cost through concerts and bake sales. The remaining cost is about the same as one new set of classroom laptops.

Cutting music would save a little money but cost students a lot. Please keep instrument lessons.$q$, 'draft')
