-- Passage rg5-authors-purpose-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b6dc7ad6-fb3a-5934-a714-c0653f14beec', 'rg5-authors-purpose-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-authors-purpose'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Tagging Monarchs at Prairie Hill$q$, $q$Account 1: Field Notes of Dr. Amara Osei, Biologist

September 14. Clear skies, light wind. Our team and twelve volunteers tagged 212 monarch butterflies today. Each tag is a tiny round sticker with a code number. We place it on the lower part of one hind wing, where it does not affect flying. Last year on this date we tagged 340. The drop may be linked to fewer milkweed plants nearby. Monarch caterpillars eat only milkweed.

Account 2: Journal of Leo Park, Age 10, Volunteer

Today I caught a real monarch with a net! Dr. Osei showed me how to hold it gently by the wings. It weighed almost nothing, like holding a leaf. I was nervous I would hurt it, but she said I was doing great. I pressed the sticker on, and then I let it go. It flew straight up over the tall grass. I hope it makes it all the way to the mountains in Mexico.

Account 3: From the County Farm Newsletter, by Rosa Delgado, Farmer

Two years ago, scientists asked if I would let milkweed grow along my fences. At first I said no. I worried the weeds would spread into my corn. Then we agreed I would mow a wide strip between the fences and the field. Now the milkweed stays put, and my fence rows are full of orange wings each fall. I am proud my farm helps the butterflies.$q$, 'draft')
